<p>In September 1915, North Dakota Governor {% person "louis-benjamin-hanna" %}Louis Benjamin Hanna{% endperson %}, a successful Red River Valley businessman with interests in lumber, grain, and banking, acquired the Inter-Ocean Hotel and renamed it the Lewis and Clark Hotel. The original structure was demolished in 1917 and replaced by a new hotel costing {% inflation 200000, 1917 %}.</p>

<p>The four-story pressed-brick building measured 150 by 130 feet and featured white enameled ornament. Hanna initially leased it to Keller &amp; Boyd, which also operated Fargo's Waldorf Hotel. Newspapers called the structure "The Pride of Mandan" and "architecturally one of the most beautiful hotels in the Northwest." It was listed in the National Register of Historic Places in 1983.</p>

<p>Architect {% person "william-j-gage" %}William J. Gage{% endperson %}, born in New York City, studied at the University of Illinois. He worked for Long, Lamoreaux and Long of Minneapolis in 1911 and Ellerbe and Round of St. Paul from 1912 to 1913. After moving to Fargo in 1913, he drafted for Haxby and Gillespie before forming a partnership with {% person "archie-ashelman" %}Archie Ashelman{% endperson %} in 1915. Gage moved to Seattle around 1919 and California in 1921. His surviving work also includes Beverly Hills City Hall.</p>

<p>A January 1930 fire damaged the rear of the hotel. Fargo architect {% person "w-e-kurke" %}W. E. Kurke{% endperson %} coordinated a 1935 repair and three-story expansion that added 10 apartments and 10 hotel rooms. The project cost {% inflation 18000, 1935 %}.</p>

